  "name": null,
  "problem": "Find the value of $f(3).$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $f(3)\\,=\\,$ \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}",
  "worked_solution": "From the fundamental theorem of calculus, we find that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-3}^{3}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =f(3)-f(-3) \\\\ {\\int }_{-3}^{3}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =f(3)-(-\\frac{5\\pi }{2}) \\\\ f(3) & =-\\frac{5\\pi }{2}+{\\int }_{-3}^{3}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe definite integral of $y={f}^{′}(x)$ over $[-3,3]$ is the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis. The corresponding region is a semicircle, so we find the area using the formula \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\frac{1}{2}\\pi {r}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\pi \\cdot {3}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{9\\pi }{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${\\int }_{-3}^{3}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x=A=\\frac{9\\pi }{2}.$ \n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(3) & =-\\frac{5\\pi }{2}+{\\int }_{-3}^{3}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{5\\pi }{2}+\\frac{9\\pi }{2} \\\\  & =2\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$",
